Section A: Agentic AI & Autonomous Clinical Workflows
[!NOTE] Module 07 covers "Agents and Tools" in a single topic (~1,200–1,800 words). Industry in 2026 has moved agentic AI from R&D into operational deployment (prior authorizations, scheduling, real-time alerting, autonomous documentation). This warrants dedicated, expanded treatment.

A.1 — Missing: Multi-Agent Orchestration in Healthcare
Gap: No topic covers multi-agent system design—how multiple specialized agents (retriever, validator, escalation agent, summariser) are orchestrated for regulated healthcare workflows, including human-in-the-loop interruption points and audit trails.

What to add to Module 07 or as Module 07B:

LangGraph / LlamaIndex Workflows / CrewAI patterns applied to clinical tasks
Agent memory: short-term (context window), long-term (vector store), episodic (structured logs)
Guardrails: output validators, Constitutional AI checks for HIPAA-relevant outputs
Multi-agent failure modes: cascading errors, circular reasoning, hallucinated tool calls
Regulatory framing: when does an autonomous agent become a SaMD?
Case study: autonomous prior-authorization agent (approval, denial, escalation paths)
Lab: Build a simple 2-agent system (extractor + validator) on a MIMIC-IV task using LangGraph
Priority: P0 | Location: Add as Topic 12 in Module 07 or a standalone Module 07B

A.2 — Missing: Agentic AI Safety, Red-Teaming, and Clinical Guardrails
Gap: Evaluation of generative models (Module 07 Topic 10) covers general hallucination rates. No topic specifically covers adversarial testing of agentic systems in clinical settings—jailbreaks, prompt injections into EHR data, refusal-to-answer calibration, and post-deployment monitoring of agent decisions.

What to add:

Clinical red-teaming taxonomy: factual hallucination, instruction injection, confidentiality leakage, unsafe medical advice
Red-team tooling: Garak, promptbench adapted for clinical LLMs
Agent-specific monitoring: decision logs, disagreement detection between agent and clinician
Regulatory expectation: FDA AI/ML action plan §4 (monitoring) applied to agents
Case study: documented failures in autonomous clinical documentation tools
Priority: P0 | Location: Add as Topic 13 in Module 07 or as a section in Module 15 (MLOps)

A.3 — Gap in Module 09: Ambient AI & Autonomous Documentation
Gap: Module 09 covers clinical NLP extensively but does not specifically address ambient AI (real-time voice-to-structured-note systems like Suki, Nuance DAX, AWS HealthScribe), which is the dominant 2026 deployment pattern for clinical AI.

What to add to Module 09:

Ambient AI architecture: ASR → diarization → entity extraction → FHIR write
Regulatory status: FDA enforcement discretion vs. SaMD determination for ambient documentation
Data quality concerns: hallucinated diagnoses propagating into EHRs, copy-forward amplification
Evaluation: SOAP note accuracy, physician acceptance rate, HIPAA/BAA requirements
Lab: Evaluate an ambient AI transcript against a ground-truth note using NER-F1 and a clinical plausibility checklist
Priority: P0 | Location: Add as Topic 15 in Module 09

